{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792510959,"nonce":"xrcavNDA+yPUIEWHyEL7qqhbAeg=","message":"Controls and scope:\n\nThe original curve fixture failed at (25,25): [128,0,128,255] rather than blue outside the circle, before implementation. The first polygon fixture incorrectly tried to merge background DynamicImage records through PrimitiveBatch::merge, which currently does not transfer background images. That unrelated fixture assumption was removed; no background merge redesign was folded into this fix.\n\nAfter the corrected five GPU cases and one CPU scratch-hash case passed, temporarily replacing the new clip payload capture with Vec::new made all six fail. Outside polygon/curve pixels became half-red, and the two polygon fill rules produced the same scratch hash. The control log is /private/tmp/blinc-rgba-clip-payload-negative.log. Source restored and formatted afterward.\n\nDynamicImage owns the existing image shader's five clip-header rows, three shape rows and optional explicitly closed polygon points. Rectangular scissors stay in clip_bounds with zero corner radii for nonrectangular shapes. Circles and ellipses keep the existing native screen frame; polygons use the inverse centered image affine and the same uniformly scaled local points as native SDF. Tight targets translate the shape frame rather than its point payload. Image-only foreground extraction/drains retain their own rows; normal image draws borrow them, tight-origin draws copy them for translation. Scratch hashes include those rows. No shader or GPU instance/primitive layout changes.\n\nThe new Vec header adds one vector to each CPU image record. Unclipped and ordinary rectangular/rounded images allocate no clip rows. Circles/ellipses retain eight vec4 rows (128 payload bytes); a polygon retains eight plus ceil((vertex_count+1)/2) rows, with its closing point included. Existing per-batch cached polygon points still coexist with these image-owned copies. No timing or allocator measurements are claimed.\n\nThis scope preserves native clip selection and transform approximations: the topmost nonrectangular clip plus the rectangular scissor, native screen-space curve handling, and local polygon framing. ClipShape::Path still takes the existing unsupported native fallback. Fade handling, stacked arbitrary shape intersections, shared ClipPath/CSS ABI changes, and cross-stream group isolation are outside this slice.","files":null}]}